    {
      "filename": "Gigantic Turtle on the Island of Santa Cruz in the Galapagos.JPG",
      "title": "File:Gigantic Turtle on the Island of Santa Cruz in the Galapagos.JPG",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Gigantic_Turtle_on_the_Island_of_Santa_Cruz_in_the_Galapagos.JPG",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gigantic_Turtle_on_the_Island_of_Santa_Cruz_in_the_Galapagos.JPG",
      "width": 4000,
      "height": 3000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "David Adam Kess",
      "description": "This is a photo that\n(Photographer, David Adam Kess) I took  this photo on the Santa Cruz Island in the Galapagos  of a World famous tortoise or Galápagos giant tortoise (Chelonoidis nigra).\n\nBelow text is from Wikipedia that describes &amp; gives a bit of history and background to the (Chelonoidis nigra).\n\nThe Galápagos tortoise or Galápagos giant tortoise (Chelonoidis nigra) is the largest living species of tortoise and the 14th-heaviest living reptile.\n\n\nThe Galápagos tortoise or Galápagos giant tortoise (Chelonoidis nigra) is the largest living species of tortoise and 10th-heaviest living reptile, reaching weights of over 400 kg (880 lb) and lengths of over 1.8 meters (5.9 ft). With life spans in the wild of over 100 years, it is one of the longest-lived vertebrates. A captive individual lived at least 170 years.\nThe tortoise is native to seven of the Galápagos Islands, a volcanic archipelago about 1,000 km (620 mi) west of the Ecuadorian mainland. Spanish explorers, who discovered the islands in the 16th century, named them after the Spanish galápago, meaning tortoise.\n\nShell size and shape vary between populations. On islands with humid highlands, the tortoises are larger, with domed shells and short necks - on islands with dry lowlands, the tortoises are smaller, with \"saddleback\" shells and long necks. Charles Darwin's observations of these differences on the second voyage of the Beagle in 1835, contributed to the development of his theory of evolution.",
      "dateCreated": "2011-12-03 16:29:43",
      "sourceType": "sibling",
      "sourceArticleId": "galapagos-islands-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "3zzs",
      "smallPath": "_m/3/z/z/s/galapagos-islands-wp/gigantic-turtle-on-the-island--small.jpg"
    },
